+# Request-Filtered Veterinarian Reads Bypass the Vet Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Every read method on `VetRepository` is cached in the `vets` cache, keyed by its arguments. The cache has no size limit and no eviction configured (see the survey's open question on vet cache invalidation).
+
+`REQ-VET-003` adds reads filtered by a specialty name taken from the request address. Any caller can send any text as that name. Caching those reads makes every distinct text a new cache entry.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache the filtered reads like the unfiltered ones.** Rejected: request-supplied text becomes an unbounded cache key, so any caller can grow the cache without limit.
+2. **Cache them behind a configured size limit.** Rejected for now: it needs JCache-provider-specific configuration the project does not use yet, for a directory of a handful of rows.
+3. **Leave the filtered reads uncached** (chosen). The `vets` cache keeps only the unfiltered reads, whose keys the application controls.
+
+## Decision
+
+A veterinarian read whose arguments carry request-supplied text is not cached. The `vets` cache holds only the unfiltered reads. A later filter on another shown attribute follows the same rule.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Cache growth stays bounded by the unfiltered reads' page count, whatever callers send.
+- Each filtered request queries the database. At directory size this costs nothing noticeable.
+- Caching filtered reads later means first giving the cache a size limit and then superseding this ADR.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Vet cache](../system-design.md#vet-cache)
